<speak>
chapter 2. the weight you never chose.
<break time="600ms"/>
<break time="1.5s" />
<break time="600ms"/>
after sophia scooped a prestigious scholarship to yale, she was certain her life would never be the same. the connections she would build! the lifetime friends she would make! the career opportunities she would find! and the chance yale offered her to study abroad, broaden her perspective on life and make her into a more well-rounded person! it was going to be epic.
<break time="600ms"/>
as the summer slowly turned into fall, sophia marked off each day on the calendar, excited for her move to connecticut. but, slowly, as the clock wound down, sophia’s excitement morphed into trepidation. from trepidation, she moved to fear and, just a week before her move, sophia was carrying a 50lb bag of dread on her back, unable to breathe. after she awoke on an ambulance stretcher, a kind weathered face informed her that she had suffered a serious panic attack, sophia was no longer able to pretend that everything was fine. acknowledging a problem existed, however, was not enough to make it go away, and sophia left the hospital that night with her bag of dread significantly heavier. on the ride home, sophia’s mom was fuming.
<break time="600ms"/>
“after all your father and i have done for you, this is how you repay us? do you know how much your father and i sacrificed to get you this far? there is no room for panic attacks at yale! you are enrolled at one of the most prestigious universities in the world, sophia! don’t blow this like you did your fifth grade piano recital. when i think about how much i spent on piano lessons only for you to freeze up on stage. only for you to give up on it completely. that’s the problem with you, sophia. i’ve been telling you since you were a child, but it’s like it goes in one ear and comes out the other. you have got to learn to commit to things if you want to be successful in life. you have to learn to give it 200% or don’t bother doing it. you can’t just quit halfway or end up in the hospital every time things get hard. life is hard. suck it up or you’ll never amount to anything.” for an eldest daughter like sophia, these words cut deeper than most. she had spent her childhood being “the strong one,” the responsible one, the example for her siblings. now, even in her moment of crisis, her mother saw only duty, performance, and sacrifice.
<break time="600ms"/>
the night before her move, sophia cried herself to sleep. what should have been one of the greatest memories of her burgeoning adulthood became a nightmare she couldn’t escape from. on campus, it seemed that every time she tried to make friends or answer a question in seminars, there was an invisible force stopping her. loudly, inside of her, she heard threats of her worthlessness being screamed at her all the time.
<break time="600ms"/>
during her second-year exams, sophia began to suffer from severe panic attacks again. it was as though the more she studied, the more she heard her mother’s voice telling her she wasn’t good enough. it wasn’t long before the panic returned.
<break time="600ms"/>
during a very important exam, sophia entered the exam hall trembling so badly that her professor asked if she was okay. although she nodded, all she could see was the faces of concern and disappointment in the audience as she froze in front of her piano. though seated in the exam hall, sophia was far away in the past, unable to play the piano for her fifth-grade recital.
<break time="600ms"/>
“do you know how much your father and i sacrificed to get you this far? there is no room for panic attacks at yale! you are enrolled at one of the most prestigious universities in the world, sophia!”
<break time="600ms"/>
her mother invaded her thoughts once more. despite sophia trying her hardest to silence that voice, it was too late. her heart was beating so loudly, she could no longer hear a thing. as her world began spinning into blackness, sophia saw her mother frowning at her sternly.
<break time="600ms"/>
thanks to the encouragement and kind words of her professor, sophia was able to complete the exam after all. still, she realized that she could no longer continue like this. what if this happened while taking more important exams in years to come? sophia had to finally face the truth. this invisible force was not going to go away until she faced the issue head-on.
<break time="600ms"/>
</speak>
